+### 6.7 Secret lint rules (L011, L014)
+
+Chunk C4 (plan D7). Files: `lint/lint.py`, `lint/secret_scan.py` (new), `defaults.toml`, `docs/harness-toml.md`, `skills/notebook/reference/errors.md`, `evals/secret-print-refused/` and the tests below. FR-14 in the lint: L011 refuses a cell that would show an env var's value, before anything is written or run. L014 hints at a shown name that says it holds a secret.
+
+| ID | Key | Default | Severity |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| L011 | `secret_print` | `"error"` | a configurable hard rule like L008–L010: `off` drops it, `hint` reports it after the run, `error` refuses the cell with E120, which counts toward `max_lint_rejects` (then E121) in the main conversation and for nh:cell-writer alike |
+| L014 | `secret_name` | `"hint"` | a hint, first in the hint order (results show only a few). `[lint] mode = "strict"` makes it an error, like every hint |
+
+**Relation to 6.8.** L011 stops the print before the cell runs, so the value never reaches the notebook's output, `.nh/outputs/` or Claude. Redaction (6.8) is the net for what gets shown anyway: a secret read from a file, a value from an earlier cell, obfuscated code, or `secret_print` set to `hint` or `off`. Neither replaces the other. Redaction knows values (`.env`, the environment, Jupyter tokens) and leaves the notebook's own output raw; L011 knows code, never a value. nh never reads an env value to lint.
+
+**The scan** (`lint/secret_scan.py`: stdlib plus `_shared/secrets.py`, importable on Python 3.11; run once per cell and cached on `_Cell`, so L011 and L014 share it)
+- It walks the statements in order, keeping a map of tainted names.
+  - A Python cell uses `cell.tree`. A Python cell magic (`%%time`, `%%capture`, …) has no tree, so the scan parses its masked text.
+  - A shell cell (`%%bash`, `%%sh`, `%%system`, `%%!`, `%%script` with a shell) is read as shell lines. Other `%%` cells (`%%sql`, `%%writefile`) are skipped.
+- A taint has a kind:
+  - **value**: one env var's value, or anything that shows it;
+  - **mapping**: every value (`os.environ`); iterating it gives only names;
+  - **items**: `.items()`; iterating gives a clean name and a tainted value.
+- It also carries the env var's name when the key is a string literal, and the variable that holds it.
+
+**Sources**
+
+| Code | Taint |
+|---|---|
+| `os.environ`, `os.environb`, `environ` | mapping |
+| `os.environ[k]`, `.get(k)`, `.pop(k)`, `.setdefault(k)` | value, named `k` when it is a literal |
+| `os.getenv(k)`, `getenv(k)`, `os.getenvb(k)` | value |
+| `dict(os.environ)`, `os.environ.copy()`, `{**os.environ}`, `os.environ \| {…}` | mapping |
+| `os.environ.values()` / `os.environ.items()` | value / items |
+| `dotenv_values(…)`, `dotenv.dotenv_values(…)` | mapping of `.env` |
+| `get_key(path, k)` imported from `dotenv`, `dotenv.get_key(path, k)` | value |
+| `os.path.expandvars("…$NAME…")` | value named NAME |
+| an assigned magic: `x = %env`, `x = %env NAME`, `x = !printenv NAME`, `x = !cat .env`, … | what the magic would show (below) |
+
+- **Import names.** `import os as o`, `from os import environ as env, getenv`, `import dotenv` and `from dotenv import dotenv_values, get_key` are followed. With no import in the cell, `os`, `environ`, `getenv` and `dotenv_values` are the real ones (an earlier cell imported them). A `def`, a `class`, an import of something else or an assignment shadows them.
+- **Taint through names.**
+  - `x = <tainted>` taints `x`. `a.b = …` taints the dotted name; `d[k] = …`, `d.update(…)` and `lst.append(…)` taint the container.
+  - `x += <tainted>` adds taint.
+  - Walrus, `for` targets and comprehension targets are followed (`for name, value in os.environ.items()` taints `value` only).
+  - Unpacking a literal tuple pairs the targets; any other tainted unpacking taints every target.
+  - A top-level assignment of something clean clears the name. Inside `if`, a loop, `try` or `match` it only adds, since the branch may not run. A loop body is scanned twice, so taint carried round the loop is seen.
+  - A function body is scanned with the outer taint minus its parameters.
+- **What keeps the taint** (these show the value):
+  - f-strings, `+`, `%` and `*`, `.format(…)`, `sep.join(…)`, a slice or subscript of a value;
+  - `a or b` (either); `a and b` (only `b`: `a` comes back only when empty); `a if c else b` (`a` or `b`, never `c`);
+  - a list, tuple, set or dict holding it, and `*`/`**` unpacking;
+  - str methods (`strip`, `upper`, `split`, `encode`, …), except those that return a bool or a number (`startswith`, `endswith`, `is…`, `count`, `find`, `index`);
+  - `str`, `repr`, `ascii`, `format`, `bytes`, `int`, `float`, `dict`;
+  - `list`, `tuple`, `set`, `sorted`, `reversed`, `iter`, `next`, `enumerate` (of a mapping they hold names only: clean);
+  - `json.dumps`, `pformat`, `pd.DataFrame`, `pd.Series`, and IPython's `Markdown`, `HTML`, `Latex`, `Pretty`, `JSON` and `Code` (they show what they wrap).
+- **Exempt (clean):**
+  - `len`, `bool`, `hash`, `id`, `type`, `isinstance`, `not`;
+  - `is (not) None`, `==`, `in` and every other comparison (`"X" in os.environ`);
+  - `os.environ.keys()`, `list(os.environ)`, `sorted(os.environ)`, `[k for k in os.environ]`;
+  - every other call. Passing a secret on shows nothing: `create_engine(url)`, `login(token=tok)`, `pd.read_csv(DATA_URL)`.
+
+**Sinks**
+
+| Sink | Why |
+|---|---|
+| The cell's last statement, when it is an expression not ended by `;` (not `pass`, `...` or `None`) | Jupyter shows its value |
+| `print`, `display`, `pprint`, `pp`, on any receiver (`rich.print`, `console.print`); not `print(…, file=f)` unless `f` is `sys.stdout` or `sys.stderr` | they show their arguments |
+| `sys.stdout.write`, `sys.stderr.write` | cheap: the same output as `print` |
+| logging calls (`debug` through `critical`, `exception`, `log`) on `logging` or on a receiver named `log`, `logger`, `…_log` or `…_logger` | cheap: WARNING and above reach the output with no setup, and a handler added later shows the rest |
+| `warnings.warn(msg)` | the warning is shown |
+| `raise E(…)`, `assert …, msg` | the traceback shows the message, and tracebacks go to Claude |
+| `os.system(…)`, and `subprocess.run`/`call`/`check_call`/`Popen(…)` without `capture_output` or `stdout=`, with a literal command the shell rules below flag | ipykernel 6 shows the child's output |
+
+A sink inside a function or class body counts: the code runs when it is called.
+
+**Magics** (the lines in `Masked.magic_lines`, raw text, continuation lines joined; each is checked where it sits, so `{name}` sees the taint so far)
+
+| Magic | Flagged |
+|---|---|
+| `%env` | alone (every env var), or one word without `=` (`%env NAME`). `%env NAME=value`, `%env NAME value` and `%set_env` set a value and are fine |
+| shell: `!cmd`, `!!cmd`, `%sx cmd`, `%system cmd`, a shell cell's lines, and `subprocess.check_output`/`getoutput` of a literal command (a source) | a command, after `sudo` and `NAME=value` words: `env` with options only; `printenv`; `set` with no arguments; `export` alone or `export -p`; `declare`/`typeset` with `-p` or `-x` and no `=`; `echo`/`printf`/`print` with `$NAME` or `${NAME}` (not `${#NAME}` or `${NAME:+…}`), or IPython's `{expr}` of a tainted name; `cat`, `head`, `tail`, `less`, `more`, `bat`, `tac`, `nl`, `strings`, `sort`, `uniq`, `xxd`, `od`, or `grep`/`egrep`/`fgrep`/`rg` reading a `.env` file (`.env`, `x.env`, `.env.local`; not `.env.example`, `.sample`, `.template`, `.dist`) |
+| `%pycat`, `%less`, `%more`, `%page` of a `.env` file | they show the file |
+| not flagged | a command whose output goes to a file (`> f`, `>> f`, `&> f`, `1> f`; `2> f` doesn't count), or that pipes into `wc` or `grep -q`/`-c`/`-l`/`-L`: only a count or a yes/no is shown |
+
+- `$NAME` names the env var NAME, or the cell's variable NAME when it is tainted (IPython fills `$name` from Python first).
+- `%time stmt` keeps `stmt` as Python (the masker does), so it is scanned as code.
+
+**Messages** (one line; the first hit, then `(+N more)`; quoted code, never a line number)
+
+| Hit | Message | Fix |
+|---|---|---|
+| a named env var | `` `print(os.environ["OPENAI_API_KEY"])` would show the value of env var `OPENAI_API_KEY`. `` | `` Check it without showing the value, e.g. `print("OPENAI_API_KEY" in os.environ)` or `print(bool(os.getenv("OPENAI_API_KEY")))`. `` |
+| a named value from `.env` | `` … would show the value of `DATA_URL` from `.env`. `` | `` … e.g. `print("DATA_URL" in env_values)` or `print(bool(env_values.get("DATA_URL")))`. `` (the variable holding the `.env` mapping, else `dotenv_values()`) |
+| through a variable | `` `print(key)` would show `key`, which holds the value of env var `OPENAI_API_KEY`. `` | as for the env var; with no literal name, `` e.g. `print(key is not None)` or `print(bool(key))`. `` |
+| every env var, or all of `.env` | `` `%env` would show every env var's value. `` / `` … every value in `.env`. `` | `` Show only the names, e.g. `sorted(os.environ)`, or check one without its value, e.g. `print("OPENAI_API_KEY" in os.environ)`. `` |
+| an unnamed value | `` … would show an env var's value. `` | the env var form, with `NAME` |
+
+- The message names the env var or the variable, never a value: nh never reads one. The code it quotes is Claude's own, and 6.8 redacts the result anyway.
+
+**L014 `secret_name`**
+- Fires when a sink above shows an identifier for which `_shared.secrets.is_secret_name` is true (6.8): a bare name, or an attribute's last name. For example `print(api_key)`, `config.OPENAI_API_KEY` as the last line, or `print(f"{db_password}")`.
+- The same expression rules decide what a sink shows. `len(api_key)`, `api_key is None`, `bool(token)` and `connect(password=db_password)` don't fire.
+- It reuses `is_secret_name`, so `tokens`, `tokenizer`, `author`, `max_tokens`, `token_count` and `SORT_KEY` don't fire.
+  - A string key (`df["token"]`) is never checked.
+  - A tokenizer's special tokens are skipped: a name whose parts hold `token` and one of bos, eos, pad, unk, sep, cls, mask, special, start or end (`print(tok.eos_token)` shows `</s>`).
+- A name L011 reports in the same cell gets no L014, while `secret_print` isn't `off`: one finding per mistake.
+- A name assigned a literal in the cell (`api_key = "sk-…"`, then `print(api_key)`) still fires: the literal may be a real key.
+- Message: `` `print(api_key)` shows `api_key`, whose name says it holds a secret. `` Fix: `` Show whether it is set instead, e.g. `print(bool(api_key))`, or leave it out of the output. ``
+- In strict mode it rejects the cell (E120) like every hint.
+
+**Known gaps** (L014 and redaction are the net):
+- **Cross-cell taint.** `key = os.environ["K"]` in one cell and `key` shown in a later one. L014 catches it when the name is secret-shaped.
+- **Obfuscation.** `getattr(os, "environ")`, `vars(os)`, `exec`/`eval`, `importlib`, `get_ipython().run_line_magic(…)`, `globals()`, `%whos` (it shows every variable's value), and automagic (`env` alone running `%env`).
+- **Secrets from elsewhere.** Python reads of files (`Path(".env").read_text()`, `open`, `json.load`, `configparser`), `getpass`, keyrings, cloud SDKs and notebook secret stores. The shell's `cat .env` is caught.
+- **Values that leave the scan.** A value passed through an unknown function (`mask(key)`, a request whose headers are shown later), drawn into a figure (`plt.title(key)`) or written to a file and shown later.
+- **Shell.** Commands built at run time, `subprocess` with a non-literal command, `awk`/`sed`/`cut` on `.env` (they can print names only), and the shell's own quoting (`'$X'` still counts).
+- **Parsing.** A Python cell nh can't parse (its L007 comes first) and a scan failure (a `RecursionError` on very deep code) drop the finding, like every lint rule: lint never breaks a write.
+
+**first-cell.md.** The credentials cell in `skills/init/reference/first-cell.md` reads `DATA_URL` from the env or `.env` and only passes it to `pd.read_csv`, so it lints clean. `test_lint_hard.py` lints every Python block of that file through the real `lint_cell` and asserts no errors and no L011 or L014.
+
+**Config and docs.**
+- `defaults.toml [lint.rules]`: `secret_print = "error"` after `markdown_output`, and `secret_name = "hint"` first among the hints.
+- `docs/harness-toml.md`: both rows, and the table's intro says what `hint` and `off` mean for L011.
+- `errors.md`: an L011 row in the E120 table, and a short hints note for L014.
+- §3.1's tables and §5's hard-rule keys list both rules.
+
+**Eval** (authored, not run): `secret-print-refused`, "check that OPENAI_API_KEY is set". Graders: no cell prints or shows the value (regex over `mock_calls`), the check uses a non-printing form, and an llm grader over the trace.
+
+**Tests:**
+- `tests/unit/test_lint_hard.py`: every source, sink and magic; taint through names, f-strings and loops; every exemption; a multi-line cell; `off`/`hint`/`error` and strict; messages with no value; first-cell.md clean.
+- `tests/unit/test_lint_hints.py`: L014 positives, the must-not-fire list, no duplicate of L011, and strict mode.
+- `tests/gateway/test_gateway.py`: `nh_add_cell` and `nh_edit_cell` refused with L011, each counted as a lint reject; the writer's add refused the same way.
+- `tests/unit/test_skill_files.py`: the eval case is enumerated with the others.
+
